1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Nepal has no shortage of talent. Yet for many young people, a good job still seems to begin with a flight abroad.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The ILO reported more than 740,000 labour permits in one fiscal year. That is permits, not unique people. We cannot solve migration with an app, but we can make one better option real.

3
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Our idea is Sajha Kaam: shared work. A Nepali worker meets a diaspora or global client through one small, clearly priced project. No endless applications; the goal is the first paid result.

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:51,000
A client posts a task with its pay, deadline, deliverables and revision limit visible before anyone applies.

5
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A worker applies with a relevant sample. The client sees the actual proof, offers a paid milestone, and both sides accept the same terms.

6
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The workroom tracks delivery, feedback and a human help route. Payment status comes from a licensed partner in a real launch. What you see today is a simulated demo, not a live wallet.

7
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:28,000
After a confirmed project, the worker can share a portable record of skills and results, and revoke access whenever they choose.

8
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:42,000
We rejected a digital “sovereign zone.” A municipality cannot create international law. Instead, a local skills hub can train workers while real banks and existing law handle the rest.

9
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:53,000
We would begin with twenty workers, five diaspora-led companies and ten small paid opportunities, then measure completed work and repeat clients.

10
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Global work should be a real choice from home. Sajha Kaam.
